The twelve patches in this directory are the image's COMPLETE delta from stock OmniGibson 3.9.1.
Applying them all, in filename order, to the base image's /behavior-src/OmniGibson reproduces the
runtime REALM is benchmarked on. Nothing else is needed -- no sibling checkout, no runtime bind.

This replaces the vendored-fork recipe (a whole omnigibson package staged out of the OG-lite
checkout by scripts/stage_oglite_for_build.sh). That recipe made every build depend on a second
repository being present, on the right branch, and clean; the two halves of REALM_LIGHT_FIX had in
fact never been committed to it, so the build it described could not succeed. The patches are
vendored here instead, and MANIFEST.sha256 is the drift guard the old recipe lacked.

DERIVATION (2026-08-27)
  stock base   BEHAVIOR-1K 26f2c7ef7b9cf96bd0414f81e1e751e493762779, OmniGibson/omnigibson,
               __version__ 3.9.1. Verified to be the SAME revision the base image installs:
               extracting /behavior-src/OmniGibson/omnigibson out of realm_og391.sif and diffing
               it against this tree yields differences in exactly the six files that image's
               patches touch, and byte-identical content in the other 232.
  fork         OG-lite 43c3c7da3b3837afa91926f6142876b84e47d35f (port-to-og391), clean.
  light fix    realm/misc/light_normalize_macros_og391.patch (second hunk only -- the first adds
               the perf macros, which the fork already carries) and
               realm/misc/light_normalize_dataset_object_og391.patch, both recovered from REALM
               history at 49907fc^. The fork carries neither.
  patches      per-file `diff -u` from the stock base to (fork + light fix).

VERIFIED
  Applying all twelve to a fresh copy of the stock base reproduces (fork + light fix)
  BYTE-FOR-BYTE, and the result satisfies MANIFEST.sha256 and all nine semantic grep guards.

WHAT THEY CHANGE
  01 envs/env_base.py            render-on-demand plumbing
  02 envs/vec_env_base.py        render-on-demand plumbing, vector path
  03 macros.py                   perf switches (ENABLE_VISUAL_UPDATES, OBJECT_STATE_UPDATE_WHITELIST,
                                 CONTACT_REPORTING_PATTERNS, PROXIMITY_GATE_*, INCREMENTAL_CONTACT_CACHE,
                                 RENDER_ON_STEP) + REALM_LIGHT_FIX, ON BY DEFAULT
  04 objects/dataset_object.py   REALM_LIGHT_FIX half two: gate the inputs:normalize write
  05 objects/usd_object.py       relaxed kinematic-tree assertions for REALM's custom robot USDs
  06 prims/entity_prim.py        relaxed assertions + place an articulation by its ROOT LINK
  07 prims/material_prim.py      OmniSurfaceMaterialPrim.preset_name default (drawer assets load)
  08 prims/rigid_prim.py         incremental contact cache
  09 prims/xform_prim.py         root-link-relative pose writes
  10 scenes/scene_base.py        z-offset: scene-file objects loaded ~100 m high in scenes idx != 0
  11 simulator.py                init-queue pruned by identity, not name; proximity gate
  12 utils/usd_utils.py          descriptor-pool raise, contact/pose read paths

  REALM_LIGHT_FIX is ON BY DEFAULT, so an image built from this recipe is 1.1.1-LIT unless a run
  sets REALM_LIGHT_FIX=0. macros.py prints the resolved state at import.

REGENERATING, if the fork moves or the base image is bumped
  Rebuild the three trees (stock base, fork, fork+light-fix), re-run the per-file `diff -u`, and
  refresh MANIFEST.sha256. The manifest is what makes drift a build failure rather than a silent
  behaviour change, so it must be regenerated in the same pass as the patches, never by hand.

  A future base image's upstream fixes to these twelve files will make the corresponding patch
  FAIL TO APPLY. That is the intended outcome: the build stops instead of silently discarding an
  upstream fix, which is what the wholesale-copy recipe did.
